      "id": 1,
      "text": "Puryear was originally named Littleton after farmer and Confederate veteran James T. Littleton, then renamed at his son's suggestion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puryear%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Louis Railway assumed control of the local line and forced a name change to avoid confusion with other towns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puryear%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Tennessee Valley Divide skirts Puryear’s western edge, with most water draining north into the Clarks River toward the Tennessee River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puryear%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Puryear sits at 36°26′41″N 88°20′2″W, a spot just north of the Kentucky border.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puryear%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Route 641 cuts through Puryear’s east side, linking it 4 miles north to Hazel, Kentucky, and 10 miles south to Paris.",
